  "query_preview": "..Visible satellite over the Great Basin shows mostly\nclear skies areawide aside from a few high clouds across the\nUintas. This is due to our synoptic setup of a ridge to our south\nand an active northern stream that is keeping us in an area of\nzonal flow resulting in warm dry days and cool nights with light\nand diurnally driven winds. The one exception to this is across\nthe western Uinta Basin and portions of the Swell that tend to get\nenhanced winds in westerly flow. This pattern will continue on\nThursday before our pattern begins to transition ahead of a cold\nfront.\n\nBy Friday a trough will start digging into the PNW with westerly\nflow aloft transitioning to southwesterly flow. This will allow\nsome mid and upper level moisture to stream back into the Great\nBasin. As upper level diffluence starts to overspread the region\nthere will be some isolated showers and thunderstorms Friday and\nSaturday, primarily across eastern portions of the CWA, however\nsome CAMs are suggesting some convection developing across\nnorthern Utah on Friday afternoon as a subtle shortwave precedes\nthe parent trough. \n\nSaturday will have the strongest winds as southwesterly winds\nenhance. Ensemble guidance gives ~50% chance of advisory levels\nwinds across areas along and west of the I-15 corridor. These\nwinds will decrease after the cold front moves through Saturday\nnight with some enhanced post frontal winds across valleys of\neastern Utah. Temperatures will fall 10-20 degrees behind the\nfront with our coldest air of the season likely by Monday morning\nwith many valley locations dipping down into the 30s and 40s.\n\nDeterministic EC and GFS both depict a rex block setting up off\nthe west coast next week that would likely keep southwesterly\nwinds in place along with warm and dry conditions.",
